    "subtopic": "Lines, angles & triangles",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the figure, segment <math alttext=\"upper A upper D\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n</mrow></math> and segment <math alttext=\"upper B upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow></math> intersect at point <math alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math>. Segment <math alttext=\"upper A upper D\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n</mrow></math> is perpendicular to line <math alttext=\"script l\"><mi mathvariant=\"script\">l</mi></math> at point <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math>. Which of the following additional pieces of information is(are) sufficient to determine that triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow></math> is congruent to triangle <math alttext=\"upper D upper E upper C\"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></math>?</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-roman;\">\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Segment <math alttext=\"upper A upper D\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n</mrow></math> is perpendicular to line <math alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi></math> at point <math alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi></math>.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 13\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math> and <math alttext=\"upper D upper C equals 13\"><mi>D</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math></li>\n</ol>",
    "type": "mcq",
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "explanation": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is correct. In the figure, it&rsquo;s given that segment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper D\"><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi></math> is perpendicular to line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"script l\"><mi mathvariant=\"script\">l</mi></math> at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math>. Statement I is that segment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper D\"><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi></math> is perpendicular to line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi></math> at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi></math>. It follows that angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B upper A upper C\"><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper E upper D upper C\"><mi>E</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>C</mi></math> are right angles. Therefore, triangles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D upper E upper C\"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></math> are right triangles. At least one pair of congruent corresponding sides is needed to determine triangle congruence by the side-angle-side (SAS) condition or the angle-side-angle (ASA) condition, so statement I is not sufficient to determine that triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is congruent to triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D upper E upper C\"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></math>. Statement II is that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 13\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D upper C equals 13\"><mi>D</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></math>. It follows that triangles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D upper E upper C\"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></math> share one pair of congruent corresponding sides. Two additional pairs of congruent corresponding parts are needed to determine triangle congruence by SAS condition, ASA condition, or side-side-side (SSS) condition, so statement II is not sufficient to determine that triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is congruent to triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D upper E upper C\"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></math>. Therefore, neither I nor II is sufficient by itself to determine that triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is congruent to triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D upper E upper C\"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>",
