    "difficulty": "hard",
    "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the figure shown, points <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi></math>, <math alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math>, <math alttext=\"upper S\"><mi>S</mi></math>, and <math alttext=\"upper T\"><mi>T</mi></math> lie on line segment <math alttext=\"upper P upper V\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>P</mi>\n\t<mi>V</mi>\n</mrow></math>, and line segment <math alttext=\"upper R upper U\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n\t<mi>U</mi>\n</mrow></math> intersects line segment <math alttext=\"upper S upper X\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>S</mi>\n\t<mi>X</mi>\n</mrow></math> at point <math alttext=\"upper W\"><mi>W</mi></math>. The measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper Q upper X\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"48 degree\"><mrow><mn>48</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, the measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper X upper Q\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"86 degree\"><mrow><mn>86</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, the measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper W upper U\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"85 degree\"><mrow><mn>85</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, and the measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper V upper T upper U\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"162 degree\"><mrow><mn>162</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure, in degrees, of <math alttext=\"angle upper T upper U upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>R</mi></math>? </p>",
    "type": "grid-in",
    "correctAnswer": "123",
    "explanation": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"123\"><mn>123</mn></math>. The triangle angle sum theorem states that the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is <math alttext=\"180\"><mn>180</mn></math> degrees. It's given that the measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper Q upper X\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"48 degree\"><mn>48</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> and the measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper X upper Q\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"86 degree\"><mn>86</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. Since points <math alttext=\"upper S\"><mi>S</mi></math>, <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi></math>, and <math alttext=\"upper X\"><mi>X</mi></math> form a triangle, it follows from the triangle angle sum theorem that the measure, in degrees, of <math alttext=\"angle upper Q upper S upper X\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>X</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"180 minus 48 minus 86\"><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>48</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>86</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"46\"><mn>46</mn></math>. It's also given that the measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper W upper U\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"85 degree\"><mn>85</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. Since <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper W upper U\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></math> and <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper W upper R\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></math> are supplementary angles, the sum of their measures is <math alttext=\"180\"><mn>180</mn></math> degrees. It follows that the measure, in degrees, of <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper W upper R\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"180 minus 85\"><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>85</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"95\"><mn>95</mn></math>. Since points <math alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math>, <math alttext=\"upper S\"><mi>S</mi></math>, and <math alttext=\"upper W\"><mi>W</mi></math> form a triangle, and <math alttext=\"angle upper R upper S upper W\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>W</mi></math> is the same angle as <math alttext=\"angle upper Q upper S upper X\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>X</mi></math>, it follows from the triangle angle sum theorem that the measure, in degrees, of <math alttext=\"angle upper W upper R upper S\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"180 minus 46 minus 95\"><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>46</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>95</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"39\"><mn>39</mn></math>. It's given that the measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper V upper T upper U\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"162 degree\"><mn>162</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. Since <math alttext=\"angle upper V upper T upper U\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></math> and <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper T upper U\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></math> are supplementary angles, the sum of their measures is <math alttext=\"180\"><mn>180</mn></math> degrees. It follows that the measure, in degrees, of <math alttext=\"angle upper S upper T upper U\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"180 minus 162\"><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>162</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn></math>. Since points <math alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math>, <math alttext=\"upper T\"><mi>T</mi></math>, and <math alttext=\"upper U\"><mi>U</mi></math> form a triangle, and <math alttext=\"angle upper U upper R upper T\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>U</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></math> is the same angle as <math alttext=\"angle upper W upper R upper S\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math>, it follows from the triangle angle sum theorem that the measure, in degrees, of <math alttext=\"angle upper T upper U upper R\"><mo>&ang;</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"180 minus 39 minus 18\"><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>39</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"123\"><mn>123</mn></math>.</p>",
